<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Qi (Huang Chao)</h1><p class="st-meta">881 CE – 884 CE · capital Chang'an</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The regime of the rebel Huang Chao, who seized Chang'an and declared himself emperor of Great Qi, dealing the Tang dynasty a blow from which it never recovered.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a>, a salt smuggler who had failed the civil service examinations, joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-xianzhi">Wang Xianzhi's</a> rebellion in 875 and led it after Wang's death in 878. His army swept through southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> in 879, then turned north, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> at the start of 881, proclaiming himself emperor of Great Qi.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> set up a court with officials, reign titles and some surrendered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> servants, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xizong-of-tang">Emperor Xizong</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>. But the regime controlled little beyond the capital region, food ran short, and executions of officials and massacres of the city's population turned the elite against it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>His general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a> defected to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> in 882, and in 883 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> cavalry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a> drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. He fled east and was killed in 884 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tai">Mount Tai</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> survived only in name, as Zhu Wen and Li Keyong became the dominant warlords.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/qi-huang-chao.jpg" alt="Qi (Huang Chao)" width="320" height="521"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%BB%83%E5%B7%A2%EF%BC%88%E6%AE%98%E5%94%90%E4%BA%94%E4%BB%A3%E5%8F%B2%E6%BC%94%E7%BE%A9%E5%82%B3%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>881 CE – 884 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chang'an</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Huang Chao</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Great Qi, Qi (881-884)</dd></dl></aside></div>
